  <page_number>63</page_number>
  <running_head>LYRICA.</running_head>
  <main_text>
    <p><i>Fratris dicta sui Castalides simul</i></p>
    <p><i>Faustis excipiunt plausibus, &amp; nouis</i></p>
    <p><i>Augent muneribus Delphica munera:</i></p>
    <p><i>Nec quicquam puero negant.</i></p>
    <p><i>Sed pallas solito sponte benignior</i></p>
    <p><i>Illum praecipuis ornat honoribus</i></p>
    <p><i>Largis nectarei viuida quum labra</i></p>
    <p><i>Succis eloquij rigat:</i></p>
    <p><i>Occultaque comes quum sapientia</i></p>
    <p><i>Ignotum per iter duxit in arduos</i></p>
    <p><i>Colles, ingenuum pectus vt optimis</i></p>
    <p><i>Illic tingeret aruibus.</i></p>
    <p><i>Quas semper placida iuncta Lubentia</i></p>
    <p><i>Illustrat varias mellifluo sinu</i></p>
    <p><i>Fundens delicias Gratia: quam decus</i></p>
    <p><i>Et circumuolitat fluor.</i></p>
    <p><i>His Beza Auspicibus protinus vuidam</i></p>
    <p><i>Latus spernit humum, nec trepidantibus</i></p>
    <p><i>Pennis, purpureum versus in alitem,</i></p>
    <p><i>Immensum secat aethera.</i></p>
    <p><i>Quem vatum Aeoliis si fidibus iuuat</i></p>
    <p><i>Decertare choros inter amabiles,</i></p>
    <p><i>Victorem Aonias Delius annuit</i></p>
    <p><i>Clarum carpere Laureas.</i></p>
    <p><i>Illi Teutonici limitis incolæ</i></p>
    <p><i>Cedunt, Erydani potor &amp; Italus:</i></p>
    <p><i>Et quos lacteolo pinguior vbere</i></p>
    <p><i>Vates Gallia nutrit.</i></p>
  </main_text>
  <notes>Show-through from the reverse page is faint in the right margin and was not transcribed. The Greek-free verse uses long-s forms rendered as plain s; some words in the lower lines are uncertain (e.g. "Immensum", "secat") and may be misread.</notes>
</page>
